Build a Python application called "NetArch Scan": a network discovery tool that scans an IP, subnet, range
or list using a pipeline of 5 agents, identifies each device (vendor, model, role), finds the links between
devices, verifies the results, and then pauses for a human to review and approve them before drawing the
final network architecture diagram and report.
It must run on Windows and Linux with Python 3.8+. Only the web UI may use Flask. The engine and agents must
use the standard library only (no Nmap, no pysnmp, no pip packages).
It must work on real networks AND against the separate "NetArch Lab" application, which simulates devices
on loopback. It learns about lab devices only by scanning them, never by reading the lab's files.

==================================================================
SHARED CONTRACT (must match NetArch Lab exactly)
==================================================================
- Lab subnet 127.20.20.0/24; port offset 20000 on every service (SNMP UDP 20161, SSH 20022, HTTP 20080 ...).
- If every target is a loopback (127.x) address, use offset 20000 automatically; otherwise offset 0.
  Allow a manual override.
- SNMPv2c read-only community (default "public").
- Lab devices expose sysDescr, sysObjectID, sysName, sysServices, sysLocation, ENTITY-MIB model,
  LLDP-MIB remote tables with the management IP encoded in lldpRemManAddr OIDs
  (...4.2.1.3.<tm>.<port>.<idx>.1.4.a.b.c.d), SSH/Telnet banners, and HTTP pages whose <title> is the
  device description.
- Optional lab info endpoint: GET <lab-url>/api/info returns
  {"running","subnet","offset","community","gateway","core_ip","devices":[{"name","ip","role","tier","snmp"}]}.
  The scanner backend fetches it with urllib (server side, so no CORS is needed).

