==================================================================
AGENT FRAMEWORK
==================================================================
1. ScanContext (one per scan job, saved as scans/<job-id>/context.json after every agent):
   - settings: target, hosts, live, offset, community, crawl, scope, max_hosts, gateway option.
   - devices: dict keyed by device key (IP, or "nbr:<name>" for neighbour-only devices). Each device has
     ip, name, mac, mac_vendor, ports, banners, http, snmp, lldp (raw neighbours), via [methods],
     vendor, model, role, tier, is_gateway, lan, confidence (0-100), issues [], evidence [],
     review_status ("pending"|"approved"|"edited"|"rejected"), human_edits {}.
   - links: dict keyed by "a|b" (sorted). Each link has a, b, a_port, b_port, source ("LLDP"|"CDP"|
     "inferred"|"manual"), seen_from ["a","b"] (which ends reported it), confidence, issues,
     review_status, human_edits.
   - agent_runs: per agent: status (pending|running|done|error|skipped), started, finished,
     duration_s, summary text, counts, and its log lines.
   - audit: list of {time, actor ("agent:<name>" or "human:<reviewer>"), action, target, before, after}.
   - Thread-safe: an RLock around every change.
2. BaseAgent: name, title, description, run(ctx, log, progress) -> summary dict. Every agent:
   - logs with its own prefix, e.g. "[Discovery] + 10.0.0.5 via Ports+ICMP";
   - only adds or updates the fields it owns;
   - writes an audit entry for every change it makes to an existing device or link;
   - can be rerun on its own (idempotent: rerunning replaces its own previous results).
3. Orchestrator:
   - Runs Discovery -> Enrichment -> Links -> Verification in a background thread, then stops at
     status "awaiting_review" and hands over to the Human-in-the-Loop agent.
   - Job status: queued, discovery, enrichment, links, verification, awaiting_review, finalizing,
     done, discarded, error.
   - If Discovery finds nothing, stop with status "empty" and skip the other agents.
   - "Rerun from agent N" reruns agent N and every agent after it, then returns to awaiting_review.
   - Only one job may be in an agent-running state at a time (HTTP 409 if busy). Jobs waiting for review
     do NOT block new scans.
   - Capture each job's print() output into its own log with a thread-local stdout router.

==================================================================
AGENT 1: DISCOVERY AGENT (scan devices)
==================================================================
Goal: find which targets are alive. Fast and light.
- Parse targets: single IP, CIDR, range (10.1.1.10-60 or a full end IP), hostname, comma/space list, @file;
  remove duplicates; refuse more than max_hosts (default 4096) with a TargetError.
- In parallel (ThreadPoolExecutor, default 128 workers), for each host:
  quick SNMP GET sysDescr; TCP connect on 22, 23, 53, 80, 443, 445, 554, 830, 1433, 3306, 3389, 5432,
  8080, 8443, 9100 (record open ports and banners); in live mode also cross-platform ping (success only if
  the output contains "ttl=").
- Live mode: read the ARP cache (/proc/net/arp or "arp -a"/"arp -an") and add targets that are only in ARP
  (via "ARP"); attach MAC addresses; mark this PC's own IP as "<hostname> (this PC)".
- Gateway: option "auto" (default for live), an IP, or "none". Detect the default gateway
  (/proc/net/route, Windows "route print -4", or "netstat -rn"). If it is among the targets, set
  is_gateway and its LAN (a target CIDR containing it, else a connected network, else a /24).
- Summary: hosts probed, live devices, devices by method, gateway found yes/no, duration.

==================================================================
AGENT 2: ENRICHMENT AGENT (scan additional information)
==================================================================
Goal: collect everything that helps identify each live device, then classify it.
- SNMP: sysDescr, sysObjectID, sysName, sysServices, sysLocation, ENTITY-MIB model (first non-empty).
- HTTP fingerprint on the first open web port (User-Agent "NetArchScan"): HTTPS with an unverified TLS
  context for 443/8443; follow one redirect (Location header, meta refresh, short JS location); capture
  <title>, the Server header and brand hints (cisco, juniper, aruba, fortigate, palo alto, pfsense,
  opnsense, sophos, mikrotik, ubiquiti, huawei, router, firewall, switch, access point, nvr/dvr,
  hikvision, dahua, camera, printer, laserjet, epson, canon, brother, synology, qnap).
- Reverse DNS (live), MAC vendor from oui.csv if present, locally administered MACs = "Private MAC".
- Classification (classify.py) -> vendor, model, role, tier, and an evidence list that records WHICH data
  decided each value (e.g. "vendor=Cisco from sysObjectID 1.3.6.1.4.1.9", "role=Printer from web title"):
  - Vendor from the sysObjectID prefix (Cisco .9, Juniper .2636, Fortinet .12356, Palo Alto .25461,
    Arista .30065, Check Point .2620, HPE .11, Aruba .14823, MikroTik .14988), else brand regexes, else the
    MAC vendor, else banners/OS text.
  - Ordered regex rules over sysDescr + model + web title/server/hints + MAC vendor:
    ex4400 -> L3 Switch Core; ex4100 -> Switch Access; ex4000 -> Switch Edge;
    c9300|cat9k_iosxe -> L3 Switch Distribution; c9200|cat9k_lite -> Switch Access; ap45|mist -> Wireless AP;
    asa|fortigate|pan-os|srx|firepower|pfsense|opnsense|sophos -> Firewall Perimeter;
    isr|asr|mx|csr1000|c8xxx|"internet router" -> Router WAN; router|mikrotik -> Router;
    access point -> Wireless AP; nvr/dvr/camera -> IP Camera / NVR; printers -> Printer;
    synology|qnap|diskstation|nas -> NAS / Storage; phone -> IP Phone; nexus|catalyst|switch -> Switch.
  - SNMP fallback on sysServices: bits 6 -> L3 Switch, 4 -> Router, 2 -> Switch.
  - Port fallback: 554 camera, 9100 printer, DB ports Database Server, web ports Web Server, RDP/SMB
    Workstation, else Host.
  - Gateway: keep its role if it is already Router, Firewall or L3 Switch; otherwise classify it as Router.
    Its tier becomes Gateway unless it is already Perimeter or Core. Use the web title as the model for
    routers/cameras/printers when there is no other model.
    Readable default names: router-1, pc-20, camera-nvr-30, printer-40.
- Apply remembered human corrections from overrides.json (matched by MAC first, then IP + sysName) and add
  evidence "override from human review on <date>".
- Summary: devices enriched, via SNMP, via HTTP, vendors found, overrides applied.

==================================================================
AGENT 3: LINK AGENT (scan links in each device)
==================================================================
Goal: find how devices connect to each other.
- For every device with SNMP, walk LLDP-MIB (local port, remote port, remote name, remote description,
  management IP) and CISCO-CDP-MIB (device ID, address decoded from 0x-hex, port, platform; local port
  from ifName). Skip duplicate neighbours.
- Match each neighbour to a known device by management IP, then by normalised name. If there is no match,
  add a neighbour-only device (via "LLDP"/"CDP") and classify it from the neighbour description.
- Crawl: follow neighbour management IPs for N hops (0-5), limited to RFC1918 + loopback or a scope list.
  New devices found by crawling go through the same Discovery probe and Enrichment classification steps.
- Build links keyed "a|b" with ports from both ends and seen_from = which ends reported the link.
- Gateway inference: every connected group of devices on the gateway's LAN that does not include the
  gateway gets ONE link (source "inferred") from the gateway to that group's highest-tier device.
- Summary: devices with neighbour tables, LLDP links, CDP links, inferred links, devices added by crawl.

==================================================================
AGENT 4: VERIFICATION AGENT (verify the devices)
==================================================================
Goal: check the results, score how much to trust each one, and flag what a human should look at.
- Device confidence score (0-100), built from the evidence:
  SNMP identity +40, ENTITY model +10, vendor from sysObjectID +15, HTTP title/brand +15, MAC vendor +5,
  open ports +5, seen in a neighbour table +10, reverse DNS +5; cap at 100. Neighbour-only devices
  start at 40. ARP-only devices start at 20.
- Device checks (each adds an issue with severity info/warning/critical):
  - Conflicting vendor between sources (e.g. sysObjectID says Cisco but the web title says Juniper).
  - Role decided only by the port fallback ("weak classification").
  - Name or model unknown.
  - Same MAC on two IPs, or the same sysName on two IPs (possible duplicate).
  - Neighbour-only device whose management IP does not answer.
  - Gateway detected but not reachable, or more than one device marked as gateway.
  - Security findings: Telnet open (critical), RDP/SMB on network devices, RTSP 554, admin web UI on
    plain HTTP for a network device.
- Re-probe: any device scoring below 50 is probed once more (SNMP + ports) with double timeouts; update
  it if the second probe adds evidence, and record "re-verified" in the audit log.
- Link checks:
  - Confirmed in both directions = confidence 100; reported by one end only = 70 with an info issue;
    port names that do not match what the other end reports = warning; inferred = 40 with an info issue
    "assumed from gateway, not measured".
  - Links to a device that no longer answers = warning.
- Set every item to review_status "pending". Mark items as "auto-approve candidates" when confidence
  >= 80 and they have no warning or critical issues.
- Optional AI explanation (OFF by default, setting "llm" in the UI): if an OpenAI-compatible endpoint is
  configured (for example a local Ollama URL or Azure OpenAI, set through environment variables), send ONLY
  the device summary and issue list (no credentials, no community string) and add a one-paragraph
  plain-English explanation per flagged device. If the call fails, continue without it.
- Summary: devices verified, average confidence, counts of critical/warning/info issues, items needing
  review, auto-approve candidates.

==================================================================
AGENT 5: HUMAN-IN-THE-LOOP AGENT (review and approve)
==================================================================
Goal: nothing is final until a person has reviewed it.
- After Verification, the job waits in status "awaiting_review". The diagram shown during review is a
  DRAFT (watermarked "DRAFT – pending review", pending items drawn with dotted outlines, rejected items
  hidden).
- The reviewer can:
  - Approve or reject each device and each link.
  - Edit a device: name, vendor, model, role, tier, gateway flag, notes. Edits set review_status "edited"
    and are written to the audit log with before/after values.
  - Add a manual link between two devices (source "manual", confidence 100) or edit link ports.
  - Bulk actions: "Approve all auto-approve candidates", "Approve all", "Reject all ARP-only devices".
  - Tick "Remember my corrections" so edits are saved to overrides.json for future scans.
  - Rerun from any agent (for example rerun Links with crawl 3) without losing approvals of items that
    still exist.
  - Discard the scan (status "discarded").
- Finalize is allowed only when there are no pending items with critical issues; otherwise show which items
  are blocking it. On Finalize:
  - Pending items are treated as approved only if the reviewer ticks "approve remaining pending items",
    otherwise Finalize is refused.
  - Rejected devices and links are left out of the final report.
  - Ask for the reviewer name (stored in the audit log; no login system).
  - Generate the final outputs (see REPORT) and set status "done".
- A finalized scan can be reopened for review, which creates a new report version (v2, v3 ...) and keeps
  the old one.

==================================================================
REPORT (report.py)
==================================================================
- Layout: a tree per connected group (gateway first, then by tier order Perimeter, Gateway/WAN, Core,
  Distribution/Network, Edge/Access, Wireless, Endpoint); isolated devices in wrapped bottom rows; non-tree
  links drawn as red dashed "redundant" lines.
- netarch_report.html (offline), titled "NetArch — Verified Network Architecture":
  header (target, mode, time, reviewer, version); KPI cards (devices, measured links, inferred links,
  manual links, network devices, vendors, average confidence, open issues); inline SVG diagram (coloured
  boxes with emoji icons by role, name / "vendor model" without repeated vendor / "IP · tier", a small
  confidence badge, orthogonal links with port labels, dashed inferred links, blue manual links, red border
  for critical issues, a pencil mark on human-edited devices, tooltips with IP/MAC/vendor/model/web
  title/via/ports/confidence/issues); legend; insights; agent pipeline summary (each agent's duration and
  summary); inventory table with confidence and review status; issues table; audit trail.
- Insights (rule-based): totals and role/vendor counts (ignore "-"); multi-vendor note; single points of
  failure when the graph is a tree; neighbour-only and ARP-only devices; randomized MAC count; network
  devices without SNMP; AP PoE uplink; security findings; when all links are inferred, explain that no
  device exposed LLDP/CDP so links were assumed from the gateway; when only the gateway was scanned,
  suggest scanning its subnet; when there are no links, suggest enabling SNMP + LLDP/CDP, crawling from a
  core switch, or including the gateway.
- Also write netarch_inventory.csv (Name, IP, MAC, MACVendor, Vendor, Model, Role, Tier, IdentifiedVia,
  OpenPorts, WebTitle, Location, Confidence, ReviewStatus, HumanEdited), netarch_links.csv,
  netarch_topology.mmd (Mermaid; "-.-" inferred, "==>" manual), netarch_scan.json (final context),
  netarch_audit.csv.

==================================================================
CLI (scan_cli.py)
==================================================================
python scan_cli.py <target> [--community] [--crawl N] [--scope CIDRs] [--gateway IP|auto|none]
  [--max-hosts] [--port-offset] [--out netarch_output] [--no-browser]
  [--review interactive|auto|skip]
- interactive (default): after Verification, print a numbered table of devices and links with confidence
  and issues, then accept commands: a <n> approve, r <n> reject, e <n> role=Router name=x, link a b,
  approve-safe, finalize, discard.
- auto: approve auto-approve candidates only, leave the rest pending, write a DRAFT report, and exit with
  code 2 if anything still needs review.
- skip: approve everything (for lab demos only; print a warning).
Print a [LIVE] approval reminder before live scans.

==================================================================
WEB APP (app.py + templates/index.html, port 5002)
==================================================================
- Page title and header: "NetArch Scan", subtitle "5-agent discovery · Human-verified architecture".
- Tab "New scan":
  - Form: target, gateway (auto / IP / none), SNMP community, crawl hops 0-5, max hosts, port offset
    (blank = auto), crawl scope, "AI explanations (optional)" toggle.
  - Lab connection card (optional): Lab URL (default http://127.0.0.1:5001), "Load from NetArch Lab"
    button using the backend /api/lab-info proxy, quick buttons "Lab subnet" and "Lab core switch + crawl"
    (core_ip, crawl 4). If the lab is not reachable, show a friendly message; scanning still works.
  - LAB/LIVE mode tag; for live targets a warning box with a REQUIRED "I have approval to scan" checkbox.
    The server also rejects live scans without approval (HTTP 400).
- Tab "Pipeline" (for the selected job):
  - A horizontal stepper with 5 agent cards (Discovery, Enrichment, Links, Verification, Human Review):
    status icon (waiting, running spinner, done tick, error, waiting-for-you), duration, key counts,
    and a "Rerun from here" button.
  - A progress bar and a dark log panel with a filter by agent, polled every 0.7s.
- Tab "Review" (enabled when the job is awaiting_review; shows a badge with the number of pending items):
  - Top: summary chips (pending, approved, edited, rejected, critical/warning/info issues), the bulk
    action buttons and "Remember my corrections".
  - Devices table: checkbox, confidence bar (red < 50, amber 50-79, green >= 80), name, IP, MAC, vendor,
    model, role, tier, via, issue icons with tooltips (and the AI explanation if enabled), review status,
    and Approve / Edit / Reject buttons. Filter: All / Needs review / Critical / Approved / Rejected.
    Edit opens a pop-up with dropdowns for role and tier and shows the evidence list.
  - Links table: device A + port, device B + port, source, seen from both ends yes/no, confidence,
    issues, Approve / Edit ports / Reject. "Add manual link" form.
  - Live draft diagram that updates after every review action.
  - Finalize panel: reviewer name, "approve remaining pending items" checkbox, Finalize button
    (disabled with the blocking reasons listed), and Discard.
- Tab "Results": KPI cards, insights, the final report in an iframe plus "Open in new tab", report version
  selector, and downloads for HTML / inventory CSV / links CSV / Mermaid / JSON / audit CSV.
- History list (left side, all tabs): time, target, mode, status pill (Awaiting review highlighted),
  device count; click to open; delete button (not while agents are running).
- Jobs are saved under scans/<job-id>/ (context.json, scan.log, report versions) and reloaded on start,
  including jobs that are still awaiting review. A job that was running when the app stopped is marked
  "error: interrupted" with a "Rerun from" option.
- REST API:
  POST /api/scan                                  start a job
  GET  /api/scans, GET/DELETE /api/scans/<id>     list, status (agent runs, counts, log), delete
  GET  /api/scans/<id>/review                     devices, links, issues, draft SVG
  PUT  /api/scans/<id>/devices/<key>              {action: approve|reject|edit, fields...}
  PUT  /api/scans/<id>/links/<key>                {action: approve|reject|edit, a_port, b_port}
  POST /api/scans/<id>/links                      add a manual link
  POST /api/scans/<id>/bulk                       {action: approve_safe|approve_all|reject_arp_only}
  POST /api/scans/<id>/rerun/<agent>              discovery|enrichment|links|verification
  POST /api/scans/<id>/finalize                   {reviewer, approve_pending, remember}
  POST /api/scans/<id>/discard, POST /api/scans/<id>/reopen
  GET  /api/lab-info?url=...                      server-side fetch, 3s timeout, http/https only
  GET  /scans/<id>/<file>                         whitelisted netarch_* files and scan.log; ?dl=1 to download
  Review actions are only allowed while the job is awaiting_review (HTTP 409 otherwise).
- Run: python app.py [--host 127.0.0.1] [--port 5002]. Bind to 127.0.0.1 by default; README notes there is
  no login, that the reviewer name is not authenticated, and that scanning requires authorisation.
- UI style matches NetArch Lab: navy/blue header gradient, white cards, pills, toasts, responsive.
  Escape all text in the HTML.

==================================================================
README
==================================================================
Setup and run; a diagram of the 5-agent pipeline and what each agent owns; the review workflow described
step by step; the confidence scoring table; how overrides.json works and how to clear it; how gateway
inference works for devices without LLDP/CDP; the optional oui.csv; the optional AI explanation settings;
security notes.

==================================================================
TESTS before finishing
==================================================================
(1) Unit tests with no lab: BER round trip; parse_targets (CIDR, range, list, too large, bad host);
    classification of sample strings (CAT9K_IOSXE -> Cisco L3 Switch, ex4100 -> Juniper Switch,
    FortiGate -> Firewall, NVR title -> IP Camera / NVR, LaserJet title -> Printer); confidence scoring;
    vendor-conflict detection; one-sided link gets confidence 70.
(2) With NetArch Lab running the campus sample: the pipeline runs agents 1-4 in order and stops at
    awaiting_review; 13 devices, 12 LLDP links confirmed from both ends where both ends have SNMP; APs are
    neighbour-only; edge-ex4000 has a critical Telnet issue that blocks Finalize until it is approved or
    rejected; scanning only 127.20.20.10 with crawl 4 also finds all 13.
(3) Gateway inference: add an unlinked printer (127.20.20.40) to the lab through its API and scan
    127.20.20.0/24 with gateway 127.20.20.10; the printer gets one inferred link to core-ex4400 with
    confidence 40, and core-ex4400 keeps its L3 Switch role.
(4) Review: editing a device's role with "Remember my corrections" writes overrides.json, and a second scan
    applies it automatically; rejected devices are missing from the final report; finalize writes the
    reviewer into the audit CSV; reopen creates report v2.
(5) Rerun from Links with crawl changed keeps existing approvals.
(6) A live scan without approval is rejected; /api/lab-info handles a stopped lab gracefully;
    --review auto exits with code 2 when items are pending.
(7) JavaScript passes "node --check".
